.TH FKREADLINE 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkreadline \- read a line from a stream into a new fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkreadline(FILE *" fp );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkreadline ()
reads one line of any length from the stream
.I fp
and returns it as a new
.BR fkstring .
.PP
The line's terminating newline is kept, so the only line returned without
one is a final line that does not end in a newline.
Embedded NUL bytes are preserved.
.PP
The stream is locked once for the whole line with
.BR flockfile (3),
and each byte is read with
.BR getc_unlocked (3).
.SH RETURN VALUE
A new
.BR fkstring ,
never empty.
.PP
.BR fkreadline ()
returns NULL if
.I fp
is NULL, or if end of file or a read error occurs before any byte is
read.
If an error occurs partway through a line, the bytes read so far are
returned; use
.BR ferror (3)
to tell this apart from a final line without a newline.
.SH ERRORS
On a read error, the error indicator of
.I fp
is set and
.I errno
is set by the underlying read.
.BR fkreadline ()
does not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
Unlike
.BR fkstrread (3),
which returns an empty
.B fkstring
at end of file,
.BR fkreadline ()
returns NULL, so that it can drive a
.B while
loop directly.
.PP
Unlike
.BR getline (3),
.BR fkreadline ()
needs no buffer from the caller: each line is a new
.B fkstring
that the caller frees with
.BR fkstrdestroy (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *line;
\&
while ((line = fkreadline(fp)) != NULL) {
    fkrtrim(line);   /* drop the newline and trailing blanks */
    process(line);
    fkstrdestroy(line);
}
if (ferror(fp))
    perror("read");
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getline (3),
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrread (3),
.BR fkslurp (3)
